A privacy technique used in online is multifactor authentication. Multifactor authentication is a combination of something a user knows, something they have, or 
something they are. This combination keeps accounts and data more secure because it makes it harder for hackers to infiltrate. 
Things you know include PINs and passwords. This is the most commonly used factor, and when used on its own is referred to as single authentication.
Things you have includes authenticator apps, SMS and emails. These all use one time passwords (OTP). This is a randomly generated code that must be entered to access 
your online account. 
Things you are include fingerprints, facial recognition and voice recognition. These are often supported by most phones, and provide an easy way to login to a device,
because they cannot be forgotten or lost, and they are hard to replicate by an attacker.
Multifactor authentication is a privacy preserving technique because it helps to keep your data private, by preventing hackers being able to gain access. With strict 
access controls like this, you remain in control of who sees your data.

